module.exports = {
  id: 'linux', title: 'Linux Commands: The Complete Toolkit', sub: '44 everyday commands, from navigation to networking and services', cat: 'Linux',
  accent: '#f5a623', icon: 'terminal', target: 49, keyword: 'LINUX', tags: '#linux #devops #sysadmin',
  scenes: [
    { type: 'hook', title: 'Linux Commands: The Complete Toolkit', kicker: 'Linux · 49 minutes', q: 'Which one command finds the process holding your port?',
      n: 'Every server, container, and cloud VM you will ever touch runs Linux, and the terminal is how you control it. In twenty minutes, we cover forty-four commands that DevOps engineers use every single day, grouped by job, with real output. Open a terminal and type along. By the end, you will know exactly which command finds the process holding your port.' },
    { type: 'roadmap', n: 'Here is the plan: how Linux boots and how the filesystem is laid out, systemd in depth, storage with LVM and filesystems, networking with ip, nftables and DNS, performance analysis, security hardening for SSH, sudo, SELinux and auditing, containers from the kernel up, and a troubleshooting playbook.' },
    { type: 'step', x: 1, chip: 'Analogy', title: 'Commands are small tools on a belt', icon: 'wrench',
      items: [['wrench', 'Each command does one job well', 'grep finds, sort sorts, wc counts'], ['link', 'Pipes connect them', 'The output of one feeds the next'], ['zap', 'Combined, they solve big problems', 'In one line, with no new software']],
      n: 'One idea to keep in mind. Linux commands are like small tools on a belt. Each one does one job well: grep finds, sort sorts, and wc counts. The pipe symbol connects them, so the output of one becomes the input of the next. Combined, they solve big problems in a single line.' },

    { sec: 'Navigation', short: 'pwd, ls, cd', title: 'Move around: pwd, ls, cd',
      cmd: ['$ pwd', '/home/dev', '$ ls -la', 'drwxr-xr-x  5 dev dev 4096 Nov  7 09:12 .', '-rw-r--r--  1 dev dev  220 Nov  7 09:12 .bashrc', 'drwxr-xr-x  3 dev dev 4096 Nov  7 09:30 projects', '$ cd /var/log && cd -', '/home/dev'],
      tip: 'cd alone goes home · cd - jumps back · Tab completes names.',
      n: 'Step one: moving around. pwd prints the directory you are in. ls lists files, dash l adds details, and dash a shows hidden dot files. cd changes directory. cd on its own goes home, and cd dash jumps back to where you were. Press Tab to complete names.' },
    { sec: 'Navigation', short: 'man, --help, type', title: 'Get help: man, --help, type',
      cmd: ['$ man ls', '$ ls --help | head -n 2', 'Usage: ls [OPTION]... [FILE]...', 'List information about the FILEs (the current directory by default).', '$ type cd', 'cd is a shell builtin', '$ which python3', '/usr/bin/python3'],
      tip: 'Inside man: / searches, n jumps to the next match, q quits.',
      n: 'Step two: getting help. man opens the manual for any command; slash searches, and q quits. Most tools also accept dash dash help. type tells you whether a command is a shell builtin or a program, and which shows where the program lives.' },
    { sec: 'Boot and layout', title: 'How Linux boots', nodes: [['power', 'Firmware', 'UEFI finds the ESP'], ['disk', 'Bootloader', 'GRUB loads kernel'], ['cpu', 'Kernel + initramfs', 'drivers, mount root'], ['gear', 'systemd (PID 1)', 'starts units'], ['terminal', 'Login', 'target reached']], edges: ['', '', 'switch_root', 'default.target'],
      cmd: ['$ systemd-analyze', 'Startup finished in 2.1s (kernel) + 6.4s (userspace) = 8.5s', '$ systemd-analyze blame | head -n 3', '$ cat /proc/cmdline'],
      tip: 'systemd-analyze critical-chain shows what delayed boot. /proc/cmdline shows how the kernel was started.',
      n: 'Step three: how Linux boots. The firmware, U E F I, finds the boot loader on the EFI system partition. G R U B loads the kernel and the initial RAM file system. The kernel starts, uses the init RAM disk to find drivers and mount the real root, then hands over to systemd as process one. systemd starts units until it reaches the default target and you can log in. systemd analyze shows where boot time went.' },
    { sec: 'Boot and layout', title: 'The filesystem hierarchy', cols: ['Path', 'Holds', 'Note'],
      rows: [['/etc', 'System configuration', 'Text files; back them up'], ['/var', 'Logs, caches, spools, databases', 'Grows; watch /var/log'], ['/usr', 'Installed programs and libraries', 'Often read-only on modern systems'], ['/home', 'User data', 'Separate partition is common'], ['/proc and /sys', 'Kernel and device views', 'Virtual: not on disk'], ['/tmp and /run', 'Temporary files', 'Cleared at boot, tmpfs']],
      tip: 'Everything is a file: even CPU and memory info are read from /proc.',
      n: 'Step four: the filesystem hierarchy. Etc holds configuration. Var holds logs, caches, and databases, and grows. Usr holds installed programs. Home holds user data. Proc and sys are virtual views of the kernel and devices, not on disk. And tmp and run hold temporary files, usually in memory. The guiding idea is that everything is a file, even CPU and memory information.' },
    { sec: 'Boot and layout', title: 'Everything is a file: peek into /proc', cmd: ['$ cat /proc/loadavg', '0.42 0.50 0.48 1/312 18734', '$ grep -E "MemTotal|MemAvailable" /proc/meminfo', '$ cat /proc/$$/status | head -n 3', '$ ls -l /proc/$$/fd | head -n 4', '$ cat /sys/class/net/eth0/operstate', 'up'],
      tip: 'MemAvailable, not MemFree, is the number to watch. The cache is reclaimable.',
      n: 'Step five: read the kernel through files. Load average, memory info, the status of your own shell, its open file descriptors, and whether a network interface is up. Tools like top and free simply format these files. When monitoring, watch memory available, not memory free, because the file cache is reclaimable.' },

    { sec: 'Files', short: 'mkdir, cp, mv, rm', title: 'Create, copy, move, delete',
      cmd: ['$ mkdir -p projects/demo/logs', '$ touch projects/demo/app.log', '$ cp -r projects/demo projects/backup', '$ mv projects/backup projects/old', '$ rm -ri projects/old', "rm: descend into directory 'projects/old'? y"],
      tip: 'There is no recycle bin. rm -i asks before each delete.',
      n: 'Step six: files and folders. mkdir dash p creates nested folders in one go. touch creates an empty file. cp dash r copies a whole folder, and mv moves or renames. rm deletes for good, because there is no recycle bin, so dash i asks before each delete.' },
    { sec: 'Files', short: 'find', title: 'Find files: find',
      cmd: ['$ find /var/log -name "*.log" -mtime -1', '/var/log/syslog.log', '/var/log/app/api.log', '$ find . -type f -size +100M', './backups/db.dump', '$ find /tmp -name "*.tmp" -mmin +60 -delete'],
      tip: 'Run without -delete first, to see exactly what would be removed.',
      n: 'Step seven: find. Search by name with a pattern, by age with mtime, where minus one means the last day, or by size, like files over a hundred megabytes. Add dash delete to remove matches, but always run it without that flag first.' },
    { sec: 'Files', short: 'ln', title: 'Links: ln and ln -s',
      items: [['link', 'Symbolic link (ln -s)', 'A shortcut to a path'], ['file', 'Hard link (ln)', 'A second name for the same data'], ['refresh', 'Classic use: current → release', 'Switch versions instantly']],
      cmd: ['$ ln -s /opt/app/releases/v42 /opt/app/current', '$ ls -l /opt/app/current', 'lrwxrwxrwx 1 dev dev 22 Nov  7 current -> /opt/app/releases/v42'],
      n: 'Step eight: links. A symbolic link, made with ln dash s, is a shortcut to a path. A hard link is a second name for the same data. Deployments often point a current link at the newest release, so switching versions is instant.' },

    { sec: 'View & search', short: 'cat, less, tail', title: 'Read files: cat, less, head, tail',
      cmd: ['$ cat /etc/hostname', 'web-01', '$ less /var/log/syslog', '$ head -n 3 access.log', '$ tail -n 50 app.log', '$ tail -f app.log', '2026-11-07 10:14:02 INFO  request served in 12ms'],
      tip: 'tail -f follows a log live. Press Ctrl+C to stop.',
      n: 'Step nine: reading files. cat prints a whole file, which suits short ones. less pages through big files, with search. head and tail show the first or last lines, and tail dash f follows a log live, as new lines arrive.' },
    { sec: 'View & search', short: 'grep', title: 'Search text: grep',
      cmd: ['$ grep -rn "ERROR" /var/log/app/', '/var/log/app/api.log:118:ERROR db timeout after 5s', '$ grep -ic "timeout" api.log', '42', '$ grep -v "DEBUG" api.log | head -n 2', '$ grep -E " 5[0-9]{2} " access.log'],
      n: 'Step ten: grep searches text. Dash r searches folders recursively, and dash n shows line numbers. Dash i ignores case, and dash c counts matches. Dash v inverts the match, to hide noise. Dash capital E enables extended patterns, like any five hundred status code.' },
    { sec: 'View & search', short: 'wc, sort, uniq', title: 'Count and rank: wc, sort, uniq',
      cmd: ['$ wc -l access.log', '18342 access.log', "$ cut -d' ' -f1 access.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -n 3", '   842 10.0.0.7', '   511 10.0.0.12', '   307 10.0.0.31'],
      tip: 'uniq only removes adjacent duplicates, so always sort first.',
      n: 'Step eleven: counting and ranking. wc dash l counts lines. This classic pipeline cuts out the first field, the client IP, sorts it, counts duplicates with uniq dash c, then sorts by count. In one line, you have the top three clients.' },

    { sec: 'Text processing', short: 'Pipes & redirects', title: 'Pipes and redirection',
      items: [['arrow', '>  write output to a file', '>> appends instead'], ['warn', '2> redirects errors', '2>&1 merges them into output'], ['link', '| pipes into the next command', 'tee writes and shows at once']],
      cmd: ['$ ls /etc > files.txt', '$ echo "done" >> files.txt', '$ ./deploy.sh > deploy.log 2>&1', '$ df -h | tee disk.txt'],
      n: 'Step twelve: pipes and redirection. One arrow writes output to a file, and two arrows append. Two, arrow redirects errors, and two, arrow, ampersand, one merges errors into the output. The pipe passes output to the next command, and tee saves it while still showing it.' },
    { sec: 'Text processing', short: 'sed', title: 'Edit streams: sed',
      cmd: ["$ sed -n '10,15p' app.log", "$ sed 's/http:/https:/g' urls.txt", "$ sed -i.bak 's/^LOG_LEVEL=.*/LOG_LEVEL=info/' app.env", '$ ls app.env*', 'app.env  app.env.bak'],
      tip: '-i edits in place. -i.bak keeps a backup first.',
      n: 'Step thirteen: sed, the stream editor. Dash n with p prints a range of lines. s, slash, old, slash, new, slash, g replaces every match. Dash i edits the file in place, and dash i dot bak keeps a backup, which is a good habit on servers.' },
    { sec: 'Text processing', short: 'awk', title: 'Columns and totals: awk',
      cmd: ["$ awk '{print $1, $9}' access.log | head -n 2", '10.0.0.7 200', '10.0.0.12 404', "$ awk -F: '$3 >= 1000 {print $1}' /etc/passwd", 'dev', "$ awk '{sum += $10} END {print sum/1024/1024 \" MB\"}' access.log", '1873.4 MB'],
      n: 'Step fourteen: awk works with columns. Dollar one is the first field, and dollar nine is the status code. Dash F sets the separator, and a condition filters rows, like human users in the password file. An END block prints totals, like megabytes served.' },
    { sec: 'Text processing', short: 'tr, xargs', title: 'Transform and fan out: tr, xargs',
      cmd: ["$ echo 'hello devops' | tr 'a-z' 'A-Z'", 'HELLO DEVOPS', '$ find . -name "*.log" -print0 | xargs -0 gzip', '$ cat hosts.txt | xargs -I{} ssh {} uptime', ' 10:21:07 up 12 days,  load average: 0.08, 0.05, 0.01'],
      n: 'Step fifteen: tr translates characters, for example lowercase to uppercase. xargs turns a list into command arguments. Pair find dash print zero with xargs dash zero, so file names with spaces stay safe. And dash capital I runs a command once per line.' },
    { sec: 'Text processing', short: 'diff, sha256sum', title: 'Compare and verify: diff, sha256sum',
      cmd: ['$ diff -u nginx.conf.old nginx.conf', '-    worker_connections 768;', '+    worker_connections 4096;', '$ sha256sum ubuntu.iso', '9f2c…a71e  ubuntu.iso', '$ sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS', 'ubuntu.iso: OK'],
      n: 'Step sixteen: compare and verify. diff dash u shows exactly what changed between two files, with minus for removed lines and plus for added ones. sha256sum fingerprints a file, and dash c checks downloads against a published list.' },

    { sec: 'Permissions', short: 'Read ls -l', title: 'Reading permissions',
      items: [['file', '- = file, d = directory, l = link'], ['user', 'rwx for the owner (user)', 'read, write, execute'], ['users', 'r-x for the group', 'members of ops'], ['lock', '--- for everyone else', 'no access at all']],
      cmd: ['$ ls -l deploy.sh', '-rwxr-x--- 1 dev ops 2.1K Nov  7 10:12 deploy.sh'],
      n: 'Step seventeen: reading permissions. The first character is the type: a dash for a file, d for a directory. Then three sets of read, write, and execute: for the owner, the group, and everyone else. Here, dev can do everything, ops can read and run it, and others get nothing.' },
    { sec: 'Permissions', short: 'chmod', title: 'Change permissions: chmod',
      items: [['hash', 'Numbers: r=4, w=2, x=1', '750 = rwx r-x ---'], ['code', 'Symbols: u g o a, with + or -', 'u+x adds execute for the owner'], ['folder', '-R applies to a whole tree', 'Use it carefully']],
      cmd: ['$ chmod 750 deploy.sh', '$ chmod u+x,g-w script.sh', '$ chmod -R o-rwx /srv/data'],
      n: 'Step eighteen: chmod. In number form, read is four, write is two, and execute is one, so seven five zero means full rights for the owner, read and run for the group, and nothing for others. Symbol form, like u plus x, changes one right at a time.' },
    { sec: 'Permissions', short: 'chown', title: 'Change owner: chown, chgrp',
      cmd: ['$ sudo chown dev:ops deploy.sh', '$ sudo chown -R www-data:www-data /var/www/site', '$ chgrp ops report.txt', '$ ls -l deploy.sh', '-rwxr-x--- 1 dev ops 2.1K Nov  7 10:12 deploy.sh'],
      n: 'Step nineteen: ownership. chown sets the owner and group together, using a colon between them. Dash capital R changes a whole folder, which is common for web roots served by the www data user. chgrp changes only the group.' },
    { sec: 'Permissions', short: 'umask, special bits', title: 'Special bits and umask',
      cmd: ['$ umask', '0022', '$ ls -ld /tmp', 'drwxrwxrwt 18 root root 4096 Nov  7 /tmp', '$ ls -l /usr/bin/passwd', '-rwsr-xr-x 1 root root 59976 /usr/bin/passwd'],
      tip: 't = sticky bit · s = setuid (runs as the file owner).',
      n: 'Step twenty: special bits. umask sets default permissions for new files. The t on slash tmp is the sticky bit: anyone can create files, but only owners can delete them. The s on passwd is setuid, so it runs as root to update password files.' },
    { sec: 'Permissions', short: 'sudo', title: 'Run as root: sudo',
      cmd: ['$ sudo -l', 'User dev may run the following commands:', '    (ALL : ALL) ALL', '$ sudo systemctl restart nginx', '$ sudo -i', '# exit', '$ sudo visudo'],
      tip: 'Always edit sudoers with visudo. It checks the syntax first.',
      n: 'Step twenty one: sudo runs one command as root, and logs it. sudo dash l lists what you are allowed to run. sudo dash i opens a full root shell, so use it sparingly. Always edit the sudoers file with visudo, which checks the syntax before saving.' },
    { sec: 'Hardening', title: 'SSH hardening', cmd: ['# /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/10-hardening.conf', 'PermitRootLogin no', 'PasswordAuthentication no', 'KbdInteractiveAuthentication no', 'PubkeyAuthentication yes', 'AllowGroups sshusers', 'MaxAuthTries 3', 'LoginGraceTime 30', 'ClientAliveInterval 300', '$ sudo sshd -t && sudo systemctl reload ssh', '$ ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -a 100 -C "kona@laptop"'],
      tip: 'Keep your current session open until a new login works. Prefer SSH certificates or Instance Connect / SSM.',
      n: 'Step twenty two: harden SSH. No root login, no passwords, public keys only, a group allow list, few authentication attempts, and a short login grace time. Test the configuration with sshd dash t before reloading, and keep your current session open until a fresh login works. Generate Ed twenty five five nineteen keys. At scale, prefer SSH certificates, or cloud tools like Session Manager and Instance Connect.' },
    { sec: 'Hardening', title: 'sudo, sudoers and least privilege', cmd: ['$ sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/deploy', '%deployers ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart shop-api, /usr/bin/systemctl status shop-api', 'Defaults log_output, use_pty', '$ sudo -l -U alice', '$ sudo journalctl _COMM=sudo --since today'],
      tip: 'Grant specific commands, never ALL. Always edit with visudo: it validates before saving.',
      n: 'Step twenty three: sudo. Grant a group exactly the commands it needs, here restarting one service, instead of all. Edit only with visudo, which validates the file before saving, since a syntax error can lock out everyone. Enable output logging, list what a user may do with sudo dash l, and review sudo activity in the journal.' },
    { sec: 'Hardening', title: 'Mandatory access control: SELinux and AppArmor', cols: ['', 'SELinux', 'AppArmor'],
      rows: [['Default on', 'RHEL, Fedora, Amazon Linux', 'Ubuntu, Debian, SUSE'], ['Model', 'Labels on everything', 'Per-program path profiles'], ['Status', 'getenforce, sestatus', 'aa-status'], ['Debug denials', 'ausearch -m avc, audit2why', 'journalctl, aa-logprof'], ['Never do', 'setenforce 0 as a "fix"', 'Disable profiles globally']],
      tip: 'A denial is information, not an obstacle. Fix the label or policy instead of turning enforcement off.',
      n: 'Step twenty four: mandatory access control. SELinux labels every file and process, and is the default on the Red Hat family. AppArmor confines programs by path profile, and is the default on Ubuntu. When something is denied, read the audit log with ausearch and audit to why, and fix the label or the policy. Setting SELinux to permissive as a fix removes a major defence.' },
    { sec: 'Hardening', title: 'Audit, fail2ban and file integrity', cmd: ['$ sudo auditctl -w /etc/passwd -p wa -k identity', '$ sudo ausearch -k identity -ts today', '$ sudo fail2ban-client status sshd', '$ sudo aide --check                     # file integrity', '$ last -n 5 && lastb -n 5              # logins and failed logins'],
      tip: 'Ship audit and auth logs off the host. An attacker who gets root can edit local logs.',
      n: 'Step twenty five: detection. The audit framework can watch files, such as the password file, and tag events with a key you can search later. Fail2ban bans sources after repeated failures. A file integrity checker, such as AIDE, detects changes to system files. Last and lastb list logins and failed logins. And ship these logs off the host, because an attacker with root can edit local files.' },
    { sec: 'Hardening', title: 'Patching and the attack surface', items: [['refresh', 'Automatic security updates', 'unattended-upgrades, dnf-automatic'], ['search', 'Kernel live patching', 'Canonical Livepatch, kpatch, KernelCare'], ['list', 'Remove what you do not use', 'ss -tlnp: every listener needs an owner'], ['lock', 'Minimal image + immutable infra', 'Replace servers instead of patching in place']],
      cmd: ['$ ss -tlnp', '$ sudo apt list --upgradable 2>/dev/null | head -n 5', '$ sudo needrestart -r l'],
      n: 'Step twenty six: patching and attack surface. Turn on automatic security updates. Use kernel live patching where reboots are hard. List every listening port with ss, and make sure each has an owner and a reason. Needrestart shows services running old libraries. And in the cloud, prefer minimal images and replacing servers over patching them in place.' },
    { sec: 'Hardening', title: 'Kernel and filesystem hardening', cols: ['Setting', 'Effect'],
      rows: [['kernel.kptr_restrict=2', 'Hide kernel addresses'], ['kernel.yama.ptrace_scope=1', 'Block ptrace of non-children'], ['net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter=1', 'Drop spoofed sources'], ['mount: noexec,nosuid,nodev on /tmp', 'No executables from temp'], ['fs.protected_symlinks=1', 'Blocks symlink races'], ['Secure Boot + module signing', 'Only trusted kernel code']],
      tip: 'Audit with lynis or a CIS benchmark scan. Apply changes in staging first.',
      n: 'Step twenty seven: kernel and mount hardening. Hide kernel pointers, restrict ptrace, drop packets with spoofed sources, and mount temporary folders without executables, set uid binaries, or device files. Secure Boot with signed modules ensures only trusted code loads. Audit the result with lynis or a C I S benchmark scan, and try changes in staging first.' },

    { sec: 'Users', short: 'useradd, usermod', title: 'Manage users and groups',
      cmd: ['$ sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash alice', '$ sudo passwd alice', '$ sudo usermod -aG docker alice', '$ getent group docker', 'docker:x:998:dev,alice', '$ sudo userdel -r bob'],
      tip: 'Use -aG. Without -a, usermod replaces all existing groups.',
      n: 'Step twenty eight: users. useradd dash m creates a home directory, and dash s sets the shell. passwd sets the password. usermod dash a capital G adds a group. Without the dash a, it replaces every existing group, a classic mistake. userdel dash r removes the user and their home.' },
    { sec: 'Users', short: 'id, who, last', title: 'Who am I, and who is here?',
      cmd: ['$ id', 'uid=1000(dev) gid=1000(dev) groups=1000(dev),27(sudo),998(docker)', '$ w', ' 10:31:44 up 12 days,  2 users,  load average: 0.12, 0.09, 0.05', 'dev      pts/0    10.0.0.4    09:58    0.00s  w', '$ last -n 2'],
      n: 'Step twenty nine: identity. id shows your user, group, and every group you belong to. w shows who is logged in and what they are running, plus the load average. last lists recent logins, which helps during security checks.' },

    { sec: 'Processes', short: 'ps, pgrep', title: 'List processes: ps, pgrep',
      cmd: ['$ ps aux --sort=-%mem | head -n 3', 'USER   PID  %CPU %MEM    RSS COMMAND', 'mysql  812   3.1 18.4 1502336 /usr/sbin/mysqld', 'dev   4410   1.2  6.0  491220 node server.js', '$ pgrep -a nginx', '901 nginx: master process /usr/sbin/nginx'],
      n: 'Step thirty: processes. ps aux lists everything running, and sorting by memory shows the biggest consumers first. pgrep dash a finds processes by name and prints their full command line, which is quicker than piping ps into grep.' },
    { sec: 'Processes', short: 'top, htop', title: 'Live view: top and htop',
      items: [['gauge', 'load average: 1, 5 and 15 minutes', 'Compare it with your CPU count'], ['cpu', 'Press P to sort by CPU, M by memory'], ['eye', 'htop: colours, scrolling, mouse', 'Install it, it is worth it']],
      cmd: ['$ top', 'top - 10:33:01 up 12 days, load average: 2.41, 1.10, 0.62', '  PID USER  %CPU %MEM COMMAND', ' 4410 dev   97.3  6.0 node', '$ htop'],
      n: 'Step thirty one: top shows processes live. The load average covers one, five, and fifteen minutes; compare it with your CPU count. Press capital P to sort by CPU, and capital M by memory. htop does the same with colours and scrolling.' },
    { sec: 'Processes', short: 'kill, signals', title: 'Signals: kill and pkill',
      cmd: ['$ kill 4410            # SIGTERM (15): ask nicely', '$ kill -9 4410         # SIGKILL: cannot be caught', '$ pkill -f "python worker.py"', '$ kill -HUP $(pgrep -o nginx)   # reload config'],
      tip: 'Send SIGTERM first so the app can clean up. Use -9 only as a last resort.',
      n: 'Step thirty two: signals. kill sends SIGTERM by default, which asks the program to clean up and exit. Dash nine sends SIGKILL, which cannot be caught, so use it last. pkill dash f matches the full command, and HUP tells many daemons to reload their config.' },
    { sec: 'Processes', short: 'jobs, nohup', title: 'Background jobs and nohup',
      cmd: ['$ sleep 300 &', '[1] 4821', '$ jobs', '[1]+  Running    sleep 300 &', '$ fg %1          # Ctrl+Z pauses, bg resumes', '$ nohup ./backup.sh > backup.log 2>&1 &'],
      tip: 'For long tasks on remote servers, use tmux so you can reconnect.',
      n: 'Step thirty three: background jobs. An ampersand runs a command in the background. jobs lists them, and fg brings one back. Control Z pauses the current job, and bg resumes it in the background. nohup keeps a job running after you log out.' },

    { sec: 'System info', short: 'uname, uptime', title: 'System info: uname, hostnamectl',
      cmd: ['$ uname -r', '6.8.0-48-generic', '$ hostnamectl', '  Static hostname: web-01', '  Operating System: Ubuntu 24.04.1 LTS', '$ cat /etc/os-release | head -n 2', '$ uptime', ' 10:35:12 up 12 days,  load average: 0.31, 0.22, 0.18'],
      n: 'Step thirty four: system information. uname dash r shows the kernel version. hostnamectl shows the hostname and operating system, and the os release file has the same details for scripts. uptime shows how long the machine has been running.' },
    { sec: 'System info', short: 'free, vmstat', title: 'Memory: free and vmstat',
      cmd: ['$ free -h', '        total   used   free  shared  buff/cache  available', 'Mem:    7.6Gi  3.1Gi  412Mi   58Mi       4.1Gi      4.2Gi', 'Swap:   2.0Gi    0B   2.0Gi', '$ vmstat 1 3'],
      tip: 'Read the available column, not free. Cache is given back when apps need it.',
      n: 'Step thirty five: memory. free dash h shows memory in human units. Do not panic at a small free number. Linux uses spare memory as disk cache, and hands it back when apps need it, so read the available column. vmstat samples memory, CPU, and swapping every second.' },
    { sec: 'System info', short: 'lscpu, lsblk', title: 'Hardware: lscpu, nproc, lsblk',
      cmd: ['$ nproc', '4', '$ lscpu | grep "Model name"', 'Model name:   Intel(R) Xeon(R) Platinum 8375C CPU @ 2.90GHz', '$ lsblk', 'NAME   SIZE TYPE MOUNTPOINT', 'nvme0n1 50G disk', '└─nvme0n1p1 50G part /'],
      n: 'Step thirty six: hardware. nproc prints the number of CPU cores, which is handy in scripts. lscpu shows the CPU model and features. lsblk draws your disks and partitions as a tree, with their mount points.' },
    { sec: 'Performance', title: 'The USE method', cols: ['Resource', 'Utilization', 'Saturation', 'Errors'],
      rows: [['CPU', 'top, mpstat', 'load avg, vmstat r', 'dmesg'], ['Memory', 'free -m', 'swap in/out, PSI', 'OOM in dmesg'], ['Disk', 'iostat -x %util', 'queue size aqu-sz', 'smartctl'], ['Network', 'ip -s link', 'drops, retransmits', 'ip -s link errors']],
      tip: 'For every resource ask three questions: how busy, how queued, any errors.',
      n: 'Step thirty seven: the U S E method. For every resource, check three things: utilization, how busy it is; saturation, how much work is waiting; and errors. For the CPU, use top and the run queue. For memory, free, swapping, and out of memory messages. For disks, iostat utilization and queue size. For the network, interface statistics, drops, and retransmits. It stops guessing.' },
    { sec: 'Performance', title: 'CPU: load average and PSI', cmd: ['$ uptime', ' 10:15:02 up 12 days,  load average: 6.20, 5.80, 4.10     # 4 cores', '$ mpstat -P ALL 1 3', '$ vmstat 1 5', '$ cat /proc/pressure/cpu', 'some avg10=12.40 avg60=9.10 avg300=5.20 total=1829384', '$ pidstat -u 1 3'],
      tip: 'Load above the core count means queueing. PSI "some" is the share of time tasks waited for CPU.',
      n: 'Step thirty eight: CPU. Load average counts running and waiting tasks, and above the number of cores means queueing. Mpstat shows each core, vmstat shows the run queue, and pidstat shows which process. Pressure stall information, in slash proc slash pressure, tells you the share of time tasks actually waited for CPU, memory, or I O, a more honest signal than load average.' },
    { sec: 'Performance', title: 'Memory: cache, swap and the OOM killer', cmd: ['$ free -h', '$ vmstat 1 5            # si/so = swap in/out: should be near 0', '$ cat /proc/pressure/memory', '$ ps -eo pid,rss,comm --sort=-rss | head -n 5', '$ sudo dmesg -T | grep -i "out of memory"', '$ systemd-cgtop -m'],
      tip: 'High "used" with low swap activity is fine. Sustained swap in/out means real memory pressure.',
      n: 'Step thirty nine: memory. Free shows used, cache, and available. Linux uses spare memory for cache, so high used is not a problem; sustained swap in and swap out in vmstat is. List the biggest processes by resident memory. If a process vanished, search dmesg for the out of memory killer. And systemd cgtop shows memory by service.' },
    { sec: 'Performance', title: 'Disk I/O', cmd: ['$ iostat -xz 1 3', 'Device   r/s   w/s  r_await w_await aqu-sz  %util', 'nvme0n1  120   340   0.4     1.2     0.9    38.0', '$ sudo iotop -oPa', '$ pidstat -d 1 3', '$ cat /proc/pressure/io'],
      tip: 'await is latency per request in ms. %util near 100 on a single HDD means saturated; NVMe handles parallelism.',
      n: 'Step forty: disk I O. Iostat extended shows requests per second, average wait in milliseconds, queue size, and utilization. Iotop and pidstat show which process is responsible. High await with a growing queue means the device cannot keep up. Note that utilization near one hundred percent means saturated on a single spinning disk, but N V M e drives handle parallel requests.' },
    { sec: 'Performance', title: 'Trace a slow process', cmd: ['$ sudo strace -f -tt -T -p 4821 -e trace=network,file -o /tmp/trace.txt', '$ strace -c -p 4821                        # syscall summary, Ctrl-C to stop', '$ sudo perf top -p 4821', '$ sudo perf record -F 99 -g -p 4821 -- sleep 30 && sudo perf report', '$ sudo ss -tp | grep 4821'],
      tip: 'strace slows the target. For production, prefer perf or eBPF tools (bpftrace, bcc, Parca).',
      n: 'Step forty one: trace a slow process. Strace shows every system call with timing, filtered to file and network calls: you will see exactly what it waits on. The dash c option summarises. Perf samples where the CPU spends time. Strace slows the process down, so on production prefer perf or e B P F tools such as bpftrace.' },
    { sec: 'Performance', title: 'eBPF observability in one slide', items: [['eye', 'bpftrace one-liners', 'Count syscalls, trace file opens, latency histograms'], ['chart', 'bcc / libbpf tools', 'execsnoop, biolatency, tcplife, opensnoop'], ['cloud', 'Continuous profiling', 'Parca, Pyroscope, Grafana Alloy'], ['shield', 'Runtime security', 'Falco, Tetragon, Tracee']],
      cmd: ['$ sudo bpftrace -e \'tracepoint:syscalls:sys_enter_openat { @[comm] = count(); }\'', '$ sudo execsnoop-bpfcc'],
      n: 'Step forty two: eBPF. It lets you run small safe programs inside the kernel to answer questions that were impossible before, with low overhead. Bpftrace one liners count system calls or trace file opens. The bcc tools show process launches, disk latency histograms, and T C P connection lifetimes. Continuous profilers and runtime security tools are built on the same foundation.' },

    { sec: 'Disk', short: 'df, du', title: 'Disk space: df and du',
      cmd: ['$ df -h /', 'Filesystem  Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on', '/dev/nvme0n1p1  49G   44G  5.0G  90% /', '$ sudo du -sh /var/log/* | sort -rh | head -n 3', '3.2G  /var/log/journal', '1.1G  /var/log/app'],
      tip: 'df -i shows inodes. A disk can be "full" with free space left.',
      n: 'Step forty three: disk space. df dash h shows how full each filesystem is. du dash s h totals folder sizes, and sorting the result finds the biggest folders fast. If writes fail with space still free, check inodes with df dash i.' },
    { sec: 'Disk', short: 'mount, fstab', title: 'Mount disks: mount and fstab',
      cmd: ['$ lsblk -f', 'nvme1n1  ext4  3f6a…c2d1', '$ sudo mount /dev/nvme1n1 /mnt/data', '$ cat /etc/fstab', 'UUID=3f6a…c2d1  /mnt/data  ext4  defaults,nofail  0  2', '$ sudo mount -a'],
      tip: 'Test with mount -a before rebooting. A bad fstab can stop boot.',
      n: 'Step forty four: mounting. lsblk dash f shows filesystems and their UUIDs. mount attaches a disk to a folder until the next reboot. For a permanent mount, add a line to fstab, using the UUID, and test it with mount dash a before you reboot.' },
    { sec: 'Storage deep', title: 'From disk to filesystem', nodes: [['disk', 'Disk / EBS / NVMe', 'lsblk'], ['layers', 'Partition (GPT)', 'parted, fdisk'], ['layers', 'LVM: PV, VG, LV', 'flexible volumes'], ['file', 'Filesystem', 'ext4 or xfs'], ['folder', 'Mount point', '/data']], edges: ['', 'pvcreate', 'mkfs', 'mount'],
      cmd: ['$ lsblk -f', '$ sudo parted /dev/nvme1n1 -- mklabel gpt mkpart data 0% 100%', '$ sudo mkfs.xfs /dev/nvme1n1p1', '$ sudo mkdir -p /data && sudo mount /dev/nvme1n1p1 /data'],
      tip: 'Mount by UUID in /etc/fstab, never by /dev/sdX name: device names can change at boot.',
      n: 'Step forty five: from disk to mounted folder. List block devices with lsblk. Create a GPT partition table and a partition. Optionally layer LVM on top for flexible volumes. Make a file system, X F S or ext four. Then mount it. In fstab, always identify devices by U U I D, never by device name, because names can change between boots.' },
    { sec: 'Storage deep', title: 'LVM: grow storage without downtime', cmd: ['$ sudo pvcreate /dev/nvme1n1 /dev/nvme2n1', '$ sudo vgcreate data_vg /dev/nvme1n1 /dev/nvme2n1', '$ sudo lvcreate -n logs -L 50G data_vg', '$ sudo mkfs.xfs /dev/data_vg/logs', '# later: grow the volume and the filesystem together', '$ sudo lvextend -r -L +20G /dev/data_vg/logs', '$ sudo lvs && sudo vgs'],
      tip: 'The -r flag resizes the filesystem too. XFS can grow but never shrink; ext4 can do both offline.',
      n: 'Step forty six: L V M. Physical volumes join a volume group, and logical volumes are carved from it. To grow a volume, extend it with the dash r flag, which also resizes the file system, while it stays mounted. Remember that X F S can grow but never shrink, while ext four can do both, offline. On cloud disks, grow the disk first, then the volume.' },
    { sec: 'Storage deep', title: 'ext4 or XFS, and what fills a disk', cols: ['', 'ext4', 'XFS'],
      rows: [['Strength', 'General purpose, shrinkable', 'Large files, parallel I/O'], ['Resize', 'Grow and shrink', 'Grow only'], ['Default on', 'Debian, Ubuntu', 'RHEL family'], ['Check', 'e2fsck', 'xfs_repair'], ['Out of inodes?', 'df -i shows it', 'Allocated dynamically']],
      tip: 'Disk "full" but df shows free space? Check inodes with df -i, and deleted-but-open files with lsof.',
      n: 'Step forty seven: choosing a file system. Ext four is a solid general purpose choice and can shrink. X F S excels at large files and parallel I O, and is the default on the Red Hat family. When a disk looks full but df shows space, check inodes with df dash i, and look for deleted files that a process still holds open, using lsof.' },
    { sec: 'Storage deep', title: 'Find what is eating the disk', cmd: ['$ df -hT | grep -v tmpfs', '$ sudo du -xh --max-depth=1 /var | sort -rh | head -n 5', '$ sudo ncdu -x /', '$ sudo lsof +L1                         # deleted but still open', '$ journalctl --disk-usage', '$ docker system df'],
      tip: 'du -x stays on one filesystem. Truncate a huge log with : > file, instead of rm, while it is open.',
      n: 'Step forty eight: find the culprit. Use df for the overview, then du with dash x, so it stays on one file system, sorted by size, or ncdu for an interactive view. Lsof with plus L one lists deleted files still held open: restarting that process frees the space. Do not forget the journal and container images. To empty a huge log that is open, truncate it rather than removing it.' },
    { sec: 'Storage deep', title: 'RAID, SMART and backups', items: [['layers', 'mdadm RAID 1/10', 'Survive a disk failure; RAID is not backup'], ['search', 'smartctl -a /dev/sda', 'Early warnings of dying disks'], ['download', 'rsync, restic, borg', 'Versioned, encrypted, deduplicated backups'], ['check', 'Test restores regularly', 'A backup never restored is a hope']],
      cmd: ['$ restic -r s3:s3.amazonaws.com/kona-backups backup /data --tag nightly', '$ restic -r s3:s3.amazonaws.com/kona-backups forget --keep-daily 7 --keep-weekly 4 --prune'],
      n: 'Step forty nine: protection. RAID keeps a server running through a disk failure, but it is not a backup, because it copies deletions and corruption too. S M A R T data warns of dying disks. For backups, restic or borg give versioned, encrypted, deduplicated copies to object storage, with retention rules. And restore regularly to prove it works.' },
    { sec: 'Disk', short: 'lsof', title: 'Open files: lsof',
      cmd: ['$ sudo lsof -i :8080', 'COMMAND  PID  USER  FD  TYPE  NODE NAME', 'node    4410  dev   21u  IPv4  TCP *:8080 (LISTEN)', '$ sudo lsof +L1 | head -n 2', 'java  2210  app  5w  REG  2.1G  /var/log/app.log (deleted)'],
      tip: 'Disk full but du disagrees? A deleted file is still open. Restart that process.',
      n: 'Step fifty: lsof lists open files, and in Linux nearly everything is a file. Dash i colon eight thousand eighty shows the process holding that port: there is your answer from the start. And plus L one finds deleted files that are still open and still using disk.' },

    { sec: 'Networking', short: 'ip', title: 'Addresses and routes: ip',
      cmd: ['$ ip -br addr', 'lo      UNKNOWN  127.0.0.1/8', 'eth0    UP       10.0.0.15/24', '$ ip route', 'default via 10.0.0.1 dev eth0', '10.0.0.0/24 dev eth0 proto kernel scope link src 10.0.0.15'],
      tip: 'ip replaces the older ifconfig and route commands.',
      n: 'Step fifty one: the ip command. ip dash b r addr gives a brief list of interfaces and addresses. ip route shows the routing table, including the default gateway, where traffic goes when no other route matches. It replaces the older ifconfig and route tools.' },
    { sec: 'Networking', short: 'ss', title: 'Listening ports: ss',
      cmd: ['$ sudo ss -tulpn', 'Netid State  Local Address:Port  Process', 'tcp   LISTEN 0.0.0.0:22          users:(("sshd",pid=702))', 'tcp   LISTEN 0.0.0.0:80          users:(("nginx",pid=901))', 'tcp   LISTEN 127.0.0.1:5432      users:(("postgres",pid=812))'],
      tip: '127.0.0.1 = local only · 0.0.0.0 = every interface.',
      n: 'Step fifty two: ss shows sockets. Dash t u l p n means TCP and UDP, listening only, with the process, in numeric form. Notice that Postgres listens on one two seven dot zero dot zero dot one, so only local apps can reach it, while nginx listens on every interface.' },
    { sec: 'Networking', short: 'ping, nc, traceroute', title: 'Test connectivity: ping, traceroute, nc',
      cmd: ['$ ping -c 3 10.0.0.1', '3 packets transmitted, 3 received, 0% packet loss', '$ traceroute example.com', '$ nc -zv db.internal 5432', 'Connection to db.internal 5432 port [tcp/postgresql] succeeded!'],
      tip: 'Many hosts block ping. nc tests the real port instead.',
      n: 'Step fifty three: connectivity. ping checks that a host answers, but many hosts block it. traceroute shows each hop along the way. nc dash z v tests whether a specific port accepts connections, which is the real question when an app cannot reach its database.' },
    { sec: 'Networking', short: 'dig, getent', title: 'DNS: dig and getent',
      cmd: ['$ dig +short example.com', '93.184.215.14', '$ dig example.com MX +short', '0 .', '$ cat /etc/resolv.conf', 'nameserver 127.0.0.53', '$ getent hosts db.internal', '10.0.0.40   db.internal'],
      n: 'Step fifty four: DNS. dig plus short returns just the answer, and you can ask for any record type, like MX for mail. resolv dot conf shows which resolver you use. getent hosts resolves names the way your apps do, including the hosts file.' },
    { sec: 'Networking', short: 'curl, wget', title: 'HTTP: curl and wget',
      cmd: ['$ curl -I https://example.com', 'HTTP/2 200', 'content-type: text/html', '$ curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code} %{time_total}s\\n" https://example.com', '200 0.184s', '$ wget -q https://example.com/app.tar.gz'],
      n: 'Step fifty five: HTTP from the terminal. curl dash capital I fetches only the headers. With dash w, curl prints the status code and total time, which makes a perfect quick health check. wget is simpler for downloading files.' },
    { sec: 'Networking', short: 'ssh', title: 'Remote login: ssh',
      cmd: ['$ ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "dev@laptop"', '$ ssh-copy-id dev@10.0.0.20', '$ ssh dev@10.0.0.20', '$ ssh -L 5432:localhost:5432 bastion', '# local port 5432 now reaches the remote database'],
      tip: 'Use keys, not passwords. Add hosts to ~/.ssh/config for short names.',
      n: 'Step fifty six: ssh. Generate a modern ed twenty-five five one nine key pair, and copy the public key to the server with ssh-copy-id. Then log in without a password. Dash capital L creates a tunnel, so a local port reaches a remote database safely.' },
    { sec: 'Networking', short: 'scp, rsync', title: 'Copy across machines: scp, rsync',
      cmd: ['$ scp app.tar.gz dev@10.0.0.20:/tmp/', '$ rsync -avz --dry-run ./site/ dev@web:/var/www/site/', '$ rsync -avz --delete ./site/ dev@web:/var/www/site/', 'sent 1.2M bytes  received 3.4K bytes  speedup is 18.73'],
      tip: 'A trailing slash on the source copies its contents, not the folder.',
      n: 'Step fifty seven: copying between machines. scp copies files over ssh. rsync copies only what changed, which is much faster for repeated syncs. Dash dash delete makes the target an exact mirror, so try dash dash dry run first.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep', title: 'ip: the one tool for addresses, routes, links', cmd: ['$ ip -br addr', 'lo      UNKNOWN  127.0.0.1/8', 'eth0    UP       10.0.1.15/24', '$ ip route', 'default via 10.0.1.1 dev eth0', '$ ip route get 8.8.8.8', '$ ip -s link show eth0              # errors and drops', '$ ip neigh                           # ARP table'],
      tip: 'ip route get shows exactly which interface and gateway a destination will use.',
      n: 'Step fifty eight: the I P command. Brief address listing, the routing table, and ip route get, which tells you exactly which interface and gateway a destination will use, the quickest way to debug routing. Link statistics show errors and drops, and ip neigh shows the A R P table. These replace the old ifconfig, route, and arp commands.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep', title: 'Firewall with nftables', cmd: ['# /etc/nftables.conf', 'table inet filter {', '  chain input {', '    type filter hook input priority 0; policy drop;', '    ct state established,related accept', '    iif lo accept', '    tcp dport { 22, 443 } accept', '    icmp type echo-request limit rate 5/second accept', '  }', '}', '$ sudo nft -c -f /etc/nftables.conf && sudo systemctl reload nftables'],
      tip: 'nft -c checks the syntax without applying it. Always keep a second SSH session open when testing.',
      n: 'Step fifty nine: a firewall with nftables. A drop policy on the input chain, then allow established connections, the loopback interface, S S H and H T T P S, and rate limited ping. The dash c flag checks syntax without applying, so you do not lock yourself out. Keep a second SSH session open while testing. Firewalld and ufw are friendlier front ends for the same kernel feature.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep', title: 'DNS on a modern system', cmd: ['$ resolvectl status | head -n 8', '$ resolvectl query api.example.com', '$ dig +short api.example.com @1.1.1.1', '$ dig api.example.com +trace | tail -n 3', '$ getent hosts api.example.com        # what applications really use', '$ cat /etc/resolv.conf'],
      tip: 'getent shows what libc resolves, including /etc/hosts. dig talks only to DNS servers.',
      n: 'Step sixty: DNS. resolvectl shows which servers and domains systemd resolved uses and can query through it. Dig asks a specific server and can trace the whole delegation. But getent hosts shows what applications really get, because it follows the same order, including the hosts file. When dig works but the app fails, compare it with getent.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep', title: 'Connection debugging ladder', nodes: [['link', 'Link up?', 'ip link'], ['globe', 'Address + route?', 'ip addr, ip route'], ['search', 'Name resolves?', 'getent, dig'], ['route', 'Port reachable?', 'nc -vz host 443'], ['lock', 'TLS + app OK?', 'curl -v, openssl']], edges: ['', '', '', ''],
      cmd: ['$ nc -vz api.example.com 443', '$ curl -sv --connect-timeout 5 https://api.example.com/healthz -o /dev/null', '$ openssl s_client -connect api.example.com:443 -servername api.example.com </dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -dates'],
      n: 'Step sixty one: a debugging ladder. Is the link up? Is there an address and a route? Does the name resolve? Is the port reachable, with nc dash v z? And do TLS and the application respond, with curl dash v and open S S L, which also shows certificate dates? Climb one rung at a time, and the first failing rung is your answer.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep', title: 'Capture traffic with tcpdump', cmd: ['$ sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -nn port 443 and host 10.0.2.9 -c 20', '$ sudo tcpdump -i any -nn -w /tmp/api.pcap port 8080', '$ tcpdump -nn -r /tmp/api.pcap | head -n 5', '$ ss -tin dst 10.0.2.9               # retransmits, rtt, cwnd'],
      tip: 'Use -nn to skip name lookups, a filter to keep captures small, and -w to open the file in Wireshark.',
      n: 'Step sixty two: packet capture. Capture on an interface with a filter by port and host, and a count, to keep it small. Write to a file and open it in Wireshark. Use dash n n to avoid name lookups. And ss with dash t i shows retransmits, round trip time, and window size for live connections, often enough to prove a network problem without a capture.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep', title: 'Kernel network tuning that matters', cols: ['sysctl', 'Why'],
      rows: [['net.core.somaxconn', 'Listen backlog for busy servers'], ['net.ipv4.ip_local_port_range', 'Outbound ports for many connections'], ['net.ipv4.tcp_tw_reuse', 'Reuse TIME_WAIT sockets (outbound)'], ['fs.file-max, LimitNOFILE', 'Open file limit per process'], ['vm.swappiness', 'Swap tendency; low for databases'], ['net.ipv4.ip_forward', 'Needed for routers and Kubernetes nodes']],
      tip: 'Put settings in /etc/sysctl.d/*.conf and apply with sysctl --system. Change one thing at a time.',
      n: 'Step sixty three: kernel tuning. A bigger listen backlog for busy servers, a wider outbound port range for many connections, open file limits, swappiness low for databases, and I P forwarding for routers and Kubernetes nodes. Keep settings in files under sysctl dot d, apply with sysctl dash dash system, and change one thing at a time, measuring before and after.' },

    { sec: 'Packages', short: 'apt, dnf', title: 'Install software: apt and dnf',
      cmd: ['$ sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y nginx', '$ apt list --installed | grep nginx', '$ sudo apt upgrade', '# Red Hat family: RHEL, Fedora, Rocky', '$ sudo dnf install -y nginx'],
      tip: 'apt update refreshes the package list. apt upgrade installs updates.',
      n: 'Step sixty four: packages. On Debian and Ubuntu, apt update refreshes the package list, and apt install adds software. apt upgrade installs available updates. On Red Hat, Fedora, and Rocky, dnf does the same job, with nearly the same commands.' },

    { sec: 'Services & logs', short: 'systemctl', title: 'Services: systemctl',
      cmd: ['$ sudo systemctl status nginx', '● nginx.service - A high performance web server', '     Active: active (running) since Fri 2026-11-07 09:01:12', '$ sudo systemctl enable --now nginx', '$ sudo systemctl reload nginx', '$ systemctl list-units --failed'],
      n: 'Step sixty five: services. systemctl status shows whether a service is running, with its latest log lines. enable dash dash now starts it and keeps it starting at boot. Reload applies config changes without dropping connections, and list units failed shows anything that broke.' },
    { sec: 'systemd deep', title: 'Write a service unit', cmd: ['# /etc/systemd/system/shop-api.service', '[Unit]', 'Description=Shop API', 'After=network-online.target', 'Wants=network-online.target', '[Service]', 'User=shop', 'ExecStart=/opt/shop/api --port 8080', 'Restart=on-failure', 'RestartSec=3', 'EnvironmentFile=/etc/shop/api.env', '[Install]', 'WantedBy=multi-user.target', '$ sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl enable --now shop-api'],
      tip: 'Never edit files under /usr/lib/systemd. Use systemctl edit for drop-in overrides.',
      n: 'Step sixty six: a service unit. The unit section says when to start: after the network is online. The service section names the user, the command, restart on failure after three seconds, and an environment file. The install section says which target wants it. After editing, reload the daemon and enable and start in one command. Never edit vendor files under usr lib; use systemctl edit for drop in overrides.' },
    { sec: 'systemd deep', title: 'Sandbox a service with systemd', cmd: ['[Service]', 'NoNewPrivileges=yes', 'ProtectSystem=strict', 'ProtectHome=yes', 'PrivateTmp=yes', 'ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/shop', 'CapabilityBoundingSet=', 'RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_INET AF_INET6', 'MemoryMax=512M', 'TasksMax=200', '$ systemd-analyze security shop-api'],
      tip: 'systemd-analyze security scores a unit out of 10. Aim to bring the exposure level down.',
      n: 'Step sixty seven: sandboxing. With a few lines, a service can be denied new privileges, given a read only view of the system, no access to home directories, a private temporary folder, no capabilities, and only network sockets. Memory and task limits stop runaways. The command systemd analyze security scores the result, so you can improve it step by step.' },
    { sec: 'systemd deep', title: 'Timers instead of cron', cmd: ['# backup.timer', '[Timer]', 'OnCalendar=*-*-* 02:00:00', 'Persistent=true', 'RandomizedDelaySec=300', '[Install]', 'WantedBy=timers.target', '$ systemctl list-timers --all', '$ systemd-analyze calendar "Mon..Fri 09:00"'],
      tip: 'Persistent=true runs a missed job after boot. Output lands in the journal, not in mail.',
      n: 'Step sixty eight: timers. A timer unit triggers a service on a calendar schedule. Persistent true runs a missed job after the machine was off, and a randomized delay spreads load. Output goes to the journal. List them with list timers, and test a calendar expression with systemd analyze calendar. Timers give you logging, dependencies, and resource limits that cron lacks.' },
    { sec: 'systemd deep', title: 'Targets, dependencies and drop-ins', cols: ['Concept', 'Meaning'],
      rows: [['Wants= / Requires=', 'Soft vs hard dependency'], ['After= / Before=', 'Ordering only, not a dependency'], ['multi-user.target', 'Text boot: servers'], ['graphical.target', 'Desktop boot'], ['systemctl edit unit', 'Creates a drop-in override'], ['systemctl cat unit', 'Shows unit plus all overrides']],
      tip: 'After= alone does not start anything. Pair it with Wants= or Requires=.',
      n: 'Step sixty nine: dependencies. Wants is a soft dependency, requires is hard. After and before only order things; they do not start anything, so pair them with wants. Targets group units: multi user for servers, graphical for desktops. systemctl edit creates a drop in override, and systemctl cat shows a unit together with all its overrides.' },
    { sec: 'systemd deep', title: 'journalctl in depth', cmd: ['$ journalctl -u shop-api --since "1 hour ago" -p warning --no-pager', '$ journalctl -u shop-api -f -o json-pretty | head', '$ journalctl -b -1 -p err          # errors from the previous boot', '$ journalctl --disk-usage', '$ sudo journalctl --vacuum-time=14d', '$ journalctl _PID=1234 _COMM=nginx'],
      tip: 'Persist logs with Storage=persistent in journald.conf, and cap size with SystemMaxUse.',
      n: 'Step seventy: the journal. Filter by unit, time, and priority. Follow live output. Read errors from the previous boot, which is invaluable after a crash. Check disk usage and vacuum old entries. And filter on any field, such as process ID or command name. To keep logs across reboots, set storage to persistent, and cap the size.' },
    { sec: 'Services & logs', short: 'journalctl', title: 'Logs: journalctl and dmesg',
      cmd: ['$ journalctl -u nginx -f', '$ journalctl -p err --since "1 hour ago"', '$ journalctl -b -1 | tail -n 20', '$ sudo dmesg -T | tail -n 2', '[Fri Nov  7 10:02:11 2026] Out of memory: Killed process 4410 (node)'],
      n: 'Step seventy one: logs. journalctl dash u shows one service, and dash f follows it. Dash p err filters to errors, and dash dash since limits the time range. Dash b minus one shows the previous boot, useful after a crash. dmesg shows kernel messages, like the out of memory killer.' },
    { sec: 'Services & logs', short: 'cron', title: 'Scheduling: cron',
      cmd: ['$ crontab -e', '# m  h  dom mon dow  command', '0  2  *   *   *    /opt/backup.sh >> /var/log/backup.log 2>&1', '*/5 *  *   *   *    /opt/healthcheck.sh', '$ crontab -l'],
      tip: 'Cron has a minimal PATH. Use full paths, and log the output.',
      n: 'Step seventy two: cron schedules jobs. Five fields set minute, hour, day of month, month, and day of week. Zero two star star star runs at two in the morning, every day. Star slash five runs every five minutes. Use full paths, and redirect output to a log.' },

    { sec: 'Archives', short: 'tar, zip', title: 'Archives: tar, gzip, zip',
      cmd: ['$ tar -czvf logs-2026-11-07.tar.gz /var/log/app', '$ tar -tzf logs-2026-11-07.tar.gz | head -n 2', '$ tar -xzf logs-2026-11-07.tar.gz -C /tmp/restore', '$ zip -r site.zip site/ && unzip -l site.zip'],
      tip: 'c = create, x = extract, t = list, z = gzip, f = file name.',
      n: 'Step seventy three: archives. tar dash c z v f creates a compressed archive. Dash t lists its contents without extracting, and dash x extracts, with dash capital C choosing the target folder. Use zip and unzip when you share files with Windows users.' },

    { sec: 'Productivity', short: 'history, alias', title: 'Work faster: history and aliases',
      cmd: ['$ history | tail -n 2', ' 1021  systemctl status nginx', ' 1022  journalctl -u nginx -f', '$ sudo !!            # rerun the last command with sudo', '$ !1021', "$ alias ll='ls -alF'"],
      tip: 'Ctrl+R searches your history as you type.',
      n: 'Step seventy four: work faster. history lists past commands. Bang bang repeats the last one, so sudo bang bang reruns it as root. Bang and a number reruns a specific entry. Control R searches your history as you type, and alias saves your favourite shortcuts.' },
    { sec: 'Productivity', short: 'env, PATH', title: 'Environment variables and PATH',
      cmd: ['$ echo $PATH', '/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin', '$ export APP_ENV=prod', '$ env | grep APP_', 'APP_ENV=prod', '$ echo \'export PATH="$HOME/bin:$PATH"\' >> ~/.bashrc', '$ source ~/.bashrc'],
      n: 'Step seventy five: environment variables. PATH is the list of folders the shell searches for commands. export sets a variable for child processes, and env lists them all. To make a change permanent, add it to your bash rc file, and source it to reload.' },
    { sec: 'Kernel containers', title: 'Namespaces: what a process can see', cmd: ['$ sudo unshare --fork --pid --mount-proc --net bash', '# ps -e          # only this shell: its own PID namespace', '# ip link        # only lo: its own network namespace', '$ lsns', '$ sudo nsenter -t 4821 -n ss -tlnp      # enter a container\'s network namespace'],
      tip: 'Docker and Kubernetes combine these: pid, net, mnt, uts, ipc, user, cgroup, time.',
      n: 'Step seventy six: namespaces. Unshare starts a shell in new process and network namespaces: ps shows only that shell, and ip link shows only the loopback. Lsns lists namespaces on the host, and nsenter enters another process namespace, a superb debugging trick for containers. Container runtimes combine process, network, mount, host name, IPC, user, and cgroup namespaces.' },
    { sec: 'Kernel containers', title: 'cgroups v2: what a process can use', cmd: ['$ cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.controllers', 'cpuset cpu io memory hugetlb pids rdma misc', '$ sudo mkdir /sys/fs/cgroup/demo && echo "200M" | sudo tee /sys/fs/cgroup/demo/memory.max', '$ echo $$ | sudo tee /sys/fs/cgroup/demo/cgroup.procs', '$ systemd-run --scope -p MemoryMax=200M -p CPUQuota=50% ./job.sh', '$ cat /sys/fs/cgroup/demo/memory.events'],
      tip: 'cgroup v2 is the default on current distributions. cgroup v1 is deprecated in Docker and Kubernetes.',
      n: 'Step seventy seven: control groups version two. One hierarchy with controllers for CPU, memory, I O, and process counts. Make a folder, write a memory limit, move your shell into it, and the kernel enforces it. Easier: systemd run starts a command with a memory max and a CPU quota. Memory events counts how often the limit was hit. Version two is the default now, and version one is deprecated in Docker and Kubernetes.' },
    { sec: 'Kernel containers', title: 'Capabilities, seccomp and the container boundary', items: [['lock', 'Capabilities', 'Split root into ~40 powers; drop all but needed'], ['filter', 'seccomp-bpf', 'Allow-list of system calls per process'], ['shield', 'LSMs: SELinux, AppArmor', 'Mandatory access policy'], ['users', 'User namespaces', 'Root in the container is nobody outside']],
      cmd: ['$ capsh --print | head -n 3', '$ grep Cap /proc/self/status', '$ getpcaps $$'],
      n: 'Step seventy eight: the rest of the boundary. Capabilities split root into about forty separate powers, so a process can bind low ports without being fully root. Seccomp is an allow list of system calls. Security modules add mandatory policy. And user namespaces make root inside the container an unprivileged user outside. Together these are what make containers a security boundary, not just a packaging format.' },
    { sec: 'Troubleshooting', title: 'A 60-second triage', cmd: ['$ uptime                         # load trend', '$ dmesg -T | tail -n 20          # kernel errors, OOM, disk faults', '$ vmstat 1 5                     # run queue, swap, CPU split', '$ mpstat -P ALL 1 3              # one hot core?', '$ pidstat 1 3                    # which process', '$ iostat -xz 1 3                 # disks', '$ free -m && ss -s               # memory, sockets', '$ df -h && df -i                 # space, inodes'],
      tip: 'Adapted from Brendan Gregg\'s "Linux Performance Analysis in 60 Seconds". Run it first on any sick host.',
      n: 'Step seventy nine: a sixty second triage for any sick host. Uptime for the load trend. Dmesg for kernel errors. Vmstat for the run queue, swap, and CPU split. Mpstat for a single hot core. Pidstat to find the process. Iostat for disks. Free and ss for memory and sockets. Df for space and inodes. Adapted from Brendan Gregg, it points you at the right resource within a minute.' },
    { sec: 'Troubleshooting', title: 'Symptom to first command', cols: ['Symptom', 'First command', 'Then'],
      rows: [['Server slow', 'uptime; vmstat 1', 'pidstat, iostat, perf'], ['Service will not start', 'systemctl status; journalctl -xeu', 'systemd-analyze verify'], ['Disk full', 'df -h; df -i', 'du -x, lsof +L1'], ['Cannot connect', 'ss -tlnp; ip route get', 'nc -vz, tcpdump'], ['Process killed', 'dmesg -T | grep -i oom', 'memory.max, free'], ['Permission denied', 'ls -l; namei -l path', 'getenforce, ausearch']],
      tip: 'namei -l /path/to/file shows the permissions of every directory on the way, a hidden cause of denials.',
      n: 'Step eighty: from symptom to first command. A slow server: uptime and vmstat. A service that will not start: systemctl status, and journalctl with x e u. A full disk: df for space and inodes, then du and lsof. Cannot connect: ss and ip route get, then nc and tcpdump. A killed process: search dmesg for the O O M killer. Permission denied: ls, and namei dash l, which shows every directory along the path.' },
    { sec: 'Troubleshooting', title: 'Linux administration checklist', items: [['lock', 'SSH keys only, sudo least-privilege, MAC enforcing', 'Audit and ship logs off host'], ['gear', 'Units with sandboxing, timers, persistent journal', 'Everything restarts and logs'], ['disk', 'UUID mounts, LVM, tested backups', 'Monitor space and inodes'], ['chart', 'USE method, PSI, 60-second triage', 'Measure, then tune'], ['refresh', 'Automatic security patches, minimal images', 'Rebuild instead of repair']],
      n: 'Step eighty one: the checklist to take away. SSH keys only, least privilege sudo, and mandatory access control enforcing. Units with sandboxing, timers, and a persistent journal. U U I D mounts, L V M, and tested backups. The U S E method, pressure stall information, and the sixty second triage. And automatic patches and minimal images, so you rebuild instead of repair.' },

    { type: 'mistakes', rows: [['rm -rf with an unchecked variable', 'Quote variables, and use ${DIR:?} guards'], ['kill -9 as the first resort', 'Send SIGTERM first'], ['Editing fstab or sudoers blind', 'Test with mount -a and use visudo'], ['chmod -R 777 to "fix" access', 'Grant the minimum with chown and chmod']],
      n: 'Four mistakes that hurt. Running rm dash r f with an unchecked variable: quote it and guard it. Reaching for kill dash nine first. Editing fstab or sudoers without testing. And chmod seven seven seven to fix access, which opens everything to everyone.' },
    { type: 'repos', repos: [['jlevy/the-art-of-command-line', 'The command line in one page', '', 'book'], ['tldr-pages/tldr', 'Short, practical man pages', '', 'list'], ['chubin/cheat.sh', 'Cheat sheets: curl cheat.sh/tar', '', 'terminal'], ['trimstray/the-book-of-secret-knowledge', 'Admin one-liners and tools', '', 'key'], ['overthewire.org/wargames/bandit', 'Learn by solving shell puzzles', '', 'globe'], ['koalaman/shellcheck', 'Find bugs in your shell scripts', '', 'bug']],
      n: 'Practice repos: the Art of Command Line, tldr pages for short examples, cheat dot sh, the Book of Secret Knowledge, the Bandit wargame from Over The Wire, which teaches through puzzles, and ShellCheck. Screenshot this frame.' },
    { type: 'recap', points: ['Boot: firmware → GRUB → kernel + initramfs → systemd', 'systemd: units, sandboxing, timers, journal', 'Storage: GPT, LVM, ext4/XFS, find what fills disks, test restores', 'Network: ip, nftables, DNS ladders, tcpdump, sysctl', 'Performance: USE method, PSI, strace/perf/eBPF', 'Hardening: SSH, sudo, SELinux/AppArmor, audit, patching', 'Containers = namespaces + cgroups v2 + capabilities + seccomp'],
      n: 'Recap. Navigate with pwd, ls, and cd. Shape text with grep, sed, awk, and pipes. Control access with chmod and sudo. Inspect processes, disks, and ports with ps, df, and ss. And remember: lsof dash i, with the port number, finds the process holding your port. Follow for more, and comment LINUX if this helped.' },
  ],
};
